[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H10SG-715
TITLE: Encrypted Local Database and Recovery Key
STATEMENT: >>>
Provider and key derivation

The embedded indoor database uses Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.Core 8.0.14 with SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlcipher 2.1.11 on the existing Windows x64 target. SQLitePCL.Batteries_V2.Init initializes the provider once before any SQLite connection. Startup requires a nonempty ``PRAGMA cipher_version`` reporting major version 4; an absent or incompatible cipher fails closed without an ordinary-SQLite fallback. The ordinary Microsoft.Data.Sqlite bundle is not shipped or initialized alongside SQLCipher. This community native bundle is not an officially supported Zetetic binary or a FIPS-validated component.

The master encryption key (MEK) consists of 16 cryptographically random bytes, providing 128 bits of key entropy. The local database password is ``Convert.ToHexString(mek)``: the uppercase 32-character hexadecimal representation supplied as an ordinary SQLCipher passphrase, not raw-key ``x'...'`` syntax and not the numeric recovery display. SQLCipher 4 derives an AES-256 key using PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 with 256000 iterations and a database salt. The design retains 4096-byte pages, per-page HMAC-SHA512, and no plaintext header. It does not reduce KDF work or disable HMAC. A 256-bit derived AES key does not increase the random MEK's 128-bit entropy.

SqlCipherConnectionFactory builds a keyed connection string once, uses ReadWrite mode and pooling for runtime connections, and sets ``PRAGMA temp_store=MEMORY`` before application SQL. Missing runtime database files are not silently recreated. Failed opens dispose their connections. Initialization and transfers use explicitly created or read-only non-pooled connections. All protobuf repositories use this factory; the host eagerly initializes every registered repository before starting its listener, preserving existing schema, CRUD, parent-column migration, and approval behavior without a separate schema registry or metadata table.

Encrypted private validation connections are opened without a connection-string Password, then keyed with a safely quoted PRAGMA key before database access. Deliberately plaintext connections omit PRAGMA key. This gives the application ownership of the native handle before a wrong key can fail schema validation; Microsoft.Data.Sqlite's connection-string password validation can otherwise fail during construction and defer handle release to garbage collection. Failed recovery or backup-password attempts therefore close their handles deterministically.

Files and the Windows protector

Paths are resolved under the configured GBSKMainSettings.StorageRoot, whose default is ``C:/HERACLES/DATA/``. The live file is ``heracles.encrypted.db``, the protector is ``heracles.db.key``, and the development-only plaintext source is ``heracles.db``. Under the default root these are ``C:/HERACLES/DATA/heracles.encrypted.db``, ``C:/HERACLES/DATA/heracles.db.key``, and ``C:/HERACLES/DATA/heracles.db`` respectively.

DpapiKeyStore uses System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData 8.0.0 to protect exactly the 16 MEK bytes with Windows DPAPI CurrentUser scope and null optional entropy. Only the binary protected blob is stored at the key path. Save writes a same-directory temporary protected blob and replaces the protector; no plaintext MEK file is written. Load uses the same scope and entropy. A missing blob, DPAPI cryptographic failure, or unprotected length other than 16 bytes means that no usable key was supplied. Access, permissions, disk, and other I/O failures instead propagate as sanitized operational startup errors, not instructions to generate a replacement key. Temporary key buffers are zeroed with CryptographicOperations.ZeroMemory; disposing SqlCipherDatabase clears its owned MEK bytes and releases its connection pool.

The recovery display

RecoveryKeyCodec splits the MEK, in byte order, into eight unsigned big-endian 16-bit integers. Each integer is multiplied by 11 and formatted as exactly six invariant-culture ASCII decimal digits; groups are joined by ASCII hyphens. For the controlled fixed vector ``00000001000A00FF01000FFF8000FFFF``, the display is ``000000-000011-000110-002805-002816-045045-360448-720885``.

Parsing trims only surrounding whitespace and requires exactly eight six-digit groups separated by hyphens, with ASCII digits only. Each value must be between 0 and 720885 inclusive and divisible by 11. Division by 11 restores the corresponding two big-endian bytes. Invalid input returns no key and is not included in an error message. This is a BitLocker-style 48-digit representation, not a BitLocker-compatible protector. The group check digits detect many transcription errors but do not authenticate a key. The numeric display is formatted on demand rather than cached.

Startup, provisioning, and recovery

1. In the UseSqliteDatabase startup branch, initialize SqlCipherDatabase and make the configured directory available before constructing the database host, channel, FieldModel, or telemetry consumers. Access failure terminates startup with a sanitized actionable message, without creating the shell. Non-SQLite branches retain their normal operation.

2. If the encrypted file exists, it is authoritative even when the development plaintext file also exists. Never generate a replacement MEK, merge, remigrate over it, or fall back to plaintext. Try the DPAPI MEK against a real ``SELECT count(*) FROM sqlite_master``. After a successful schema read, require ``PRAGMA cipher_integrity_check`` to return zero rows and ``PRAGMA integrity_check`` to return exactly ``ok``. A subsequent integrity failure is corruption: stop rather than offering overwrite or reinitialization.

3. If DPAPI supplies no usable MEK, or its MEK fails the initial schema read, request the numeric recovery key. Malformed input and a validly formatted but incorrect key receive a generic retry message; cancellation stops startup. The first prompt has no validation error. Test every candidate against the existing file and both full integrity checks before saving a new CurrentUser protector. Recovery attempts never change database bytes. On success the recovered MEK restores automatic unlock; it is not rotated.

4. If no encrypted database exists, reuse a valid DPAPI MEK left by an interrupted first initialization, or generate 16 bytes using RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes. Before committing a new database or protector, display its formatted recovery key and require the explicit acknowledgement ``I have saved this recovery key``. Cancel leaves the development source untouched. Provisioning is an operator workflow before login, because application authentication depends on database access; it is distinct from the administrator-only Settings reveal.

5. Only after acknowledgement, save the DPAPI protector. If the development plaintext source exists, use the same keyed-copy primitive as database transfer: create the encrypted output and copy all source schema and data with sqlcipher_export, using an explicitly empty source key. Do not use PRAGMA rekey. Preserve IDs, hierarchy, indexes, triggers, database metadata, and AUTOINCREMENT high-water marks. Validate the reopened encrypted output and close all source and destination connections before deleting the development plaintext file and its own SQLite sidecars. Do not retain a plaintext backup. Without a plaintext source, use a non-pooled keyed Create connection and VACUUM to create a valid empty encrypted database; normal host initialization supplies its tables.

6. A failed migration removes only its incomplete output and retains the development source. No migration journal, rollback snapshot, or resumption protocol is introduced. A leftover protector is reused and shown again at the next no-database startup. Failure to delete a successfully migrated plaintext source prevents normal startup. A subsequent startup first fully validates the authoritative encrypted file, then retries cleanup of the remaining legacy source and sidecars. Deletion is not a secure-erasure guarantee.

The standalone pre-shell recovery window uses the shared WPF resources. Provisioning shows a selectable read-only key, acknowledgement, Continue, and Cancel; recovery shows key entry, Unlock, Cancel, and a local sanitized validation message. Closing either mode cancels. Explicit WPF shutdown mode is temporarily used while the window is displayed and then restored. The key never appears in a generic exception dialog. Exit cleanup tolerates partial initialization and disposes only objects actually initialized, without resolving unused database consumers.

Security boundaries

Possession of the MEK is the recovery authority before database-backed login is available. A copied encrypted database and its saved numeric MEK can be recovered under another Windows account or machine, creating a protector bound to that account; the copied CurrentUser blob alone cannot perform that unlock. Saving the recovery key externally is the provisioning operator's responsibility; the application does not automatically copy, print, or export it as plaintext.

Cryptographic page integrity detects unauthorized alteration but does not provide rollback or replay protection against replacement with an older authentic database. Application-role controls do not isolate keys from a process already running as the provisioning Windows account. SQLCipher diagnostics, SQL, connection strings, DPAPI buffers, passwords, and recovery keys are excluded from application logs, audit text, generic exception details, and command lines. Managed strings cannot be guaranteed erased and are retained only for the operation that needs them.
<<<
