[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H10SG-716
TITLE: Administrator Data Management
STATEMENT: >>>
Availability, authorization, and secret display

Settings > Data Management retains the existing Settings/SystemSettings permission gate. Embedded-mode administrators with an active, unlocked, unexpired session can reveal the MEK recovery key, export, or import. DataManagementService is registered in every database mode, with embedded database and host objects optional; non-embedded mode reports unavailability and supplies no usable management controls. A non-administrator with Settings access sees an administrator-required message, not key or transfer controls.

Every service entry independently checks embedded availability, a non-null authorized user with Role.Name exactly equal to UserRole.BuiltInNames.Administrator, and a non-null IBearerTokenUserSessionManager.UserSession that is neither locked nor expired. UI command state uses the same predicate, with authorized-user and session-change notifications marshalled to the dispatcher. Retaining an AuthorizedUser after session expiry does not authorize a management call. No new role, permission field, credential reauthentication dialog, or management gRPC endpoint is introduced.

The MEK recovery key TextBox is read-only and initially contains only a fixed mask. A single eye-icon toggle reveals or hides it. Reveal obtains the display only after authorization; Hide removes the actual bound string rather than concealing it with color. Hide, view unload or tab departure, logout or user switch, lock or expiration, and the start of a transfer clear the revealed value. Returning to the tab always shows the mask. View-lifetime subscriptions are released appropriately. Recovery-key disclosure after login is administrator-only, separate from first provisioning and possession-based startup recovery.

A second read-only, selectable field beneath the recovery key exposes the 32-character uppercase hexadecimal MEK with the visible label and accessibility name ``Passphrase``. Administrators obtain this value through Settings > Data Management using the shared eye toggle and copy it securely for live database inspection; no manual conversion of the numeric recovery key is required. Use it as an ordinary passphrase with SQLCipher 4 settings, not as a derived raw encryption key or raw-key-mode value. The numeric recovery key remains the startup recovery credential, while an encrypted export uses its independently chosen export password rather than the local Passphrase. The Passphrase is obtained through the same administrator/session and busy-state guards and cleared back to its own fixed mask on every recovery-key hiding transition. Copying is a deliberate standard text-selection action; no automatic clipboard write is performed. Inspection procedures clear any controlled clipboard and secret input afterward and exclude secrets from logs, command lines, screenshots, and public evidence.

The nested settings view follows the existing Prism AutoWire pattern and uses BindableBase and DelegateCommand with explicit busy state and caught asynchronous errors. New settings and recovery surfaces use the application's existing shared theme and control styles. The password dialog uses DialogViewModelBase and PasswordBox controls, not the account-approval dialog or its error logging. Export requires matching confirmation; exactly empty password and confirmation select an unencrypted export. Import asks once and permits an empty password for plaintext input. Labels and help text do not instruct users to leave password fields empty. Nonempty whitespace-only passwords and embedded NUL are rejected locally; encryption passwords are used exactly as entered, without trimming, normalization, or extra complexity rules. Invalid or mismatched input keeps the dialog open with a local message. The password result is consumed immediately, and PasswordBox, view-model, and dialog-result references are cleared on every close path and in operation cleanup. No automatic clipboard action is performed.

Export uses a SaveFileDialog with overwrite confirmation, default extension .db, filter ``Database (*.db)|*.db``, and default filename ``heracles-backup-yyyyMMdd-HHmmss.db``. Import uses an OpenFileDialog with the same database filter, file-existence checking and single selection. Cancelling a file or password dialog is a no-op. Before import preparation, require explicit confirmation: ``Import replaces the entire database, including users and settings, and closes the application. Continue?``

Database snapshot transfer

SqlCipherDatabase runs synchronous SQLCipher copy and validation work on a worker task. One per-instance semaphore serializes transfers, and the UI rejects duplicate management actions while busy. A prepared import retains that reservation until install or discard; failed preparation releases it. Pre-install refusal, cancellation, or error removes only this instance's owned staging and releases the reservation. Cancellation is allowed before replacement; once replacement begins, finish the outcome and close rather than reporting an uncertain cancellation.

One private keyed-copy primitive implements development migration, export, and import. It creates a new non-pooled destination as the main database, encrypted except for an empty-password export. It then attaches the existing source through a bound, properly escaped absolute file URI with mode=ro and an explicit bound key. Development migration and plaintext imports use an empty source key. It validates and reads the source within one explicit read snapshot and invokes ``SELECT sqlcipher_export('main', 'source')``. This copies complete schema, tables, indexes, triggers, and data, not raw live bytes or selected JSON records.

The primitive preserves user_version, application_id, auto_vacuum, and sqlite_sequence high-water marks, including deleted-high-ID cases. It sets auto_vacuum before populating the destination and explicitly copies sequence contents where needed. Source schema access and source-alias integrity PRAGMAs run within the read snapshot. Plaintext sources require SQLite structural integrity but have no SQLCipher HMAC to validate; encrypted sources require both checks. Filename and ATTACH-key values are bound. A required PRAGMA literal is obtained through parameterized ``SELECT quote($value)``, not manual quoting or user-path SQL concatenation.

Temporary SQL data stays in memory. Migration outputs, import staging, and nonempty-password export outputs are encrypted throughout. Only an explicitly empty-password export uses a plaintext output and plaintext sibling temporary file. Connections are detached, checkpointed as needed, and closed. Each output is a self-contained DELETE-journal database, not a file depending on unexported WAL content. Reopening must pass schema access and integrity_check with exactly ok before success; encrypted outputs must additionally pass cipher_integrity_check with zero rows and must not have a plaintext SQLite header. Plaintext outputs are ordinary SQLite databases, with no SQLCipher header or cryptographic integrity requirement.

With a nonempty password, an export is a standalone SQLCipher 4 database protected only by the exact user-supplied export password with a new salt. With an exactly empty password, it is an ordinary unencrypted SQLite database readable without a key; this deliberate disclosure does not decrypt or otherwise change the encrypted live database or local key protector. Neither format contains a MEK wrapper, custom archive, JSON-only subset, or companion key file. Each export is written to a unique sibling of the chosen destination in its selected format. Only after complete validation is an existing destination replaced, using file replacement rather than writing through a link. Failure or cancellation leaves an existing destination intact and removes incomplete owned output. Normal database use continues during export; the read snapshot defines one consistent point in time. Files such as external photos and application logs are outside this database-only operation.

Import preparation opens the selected source read-only. An ordinary SQLite header selects plaintext handling without applying a source password; other inputs use the supplied SQLCipher password. Missing or empty files, non-databases, incorrect encryption passwords, cryptographic or structural corruption, and incompatible schema are rejected before affecting live data. Plaintext structural checks cannot authenticate the source or detect every semantically valid alteration. Compatibility is measured against all non-sqlite_% tables in the fully initialized live schema: every live table and column must exist with matching declared type, NOT NULL, and primary-key flags. Extra source tables and columns are accepted and preserved. Repository auto-migrations are not run against the source, and imported treatment loaded-state values are not reset.

An accepted import snapshot is re-encrypted into a unique staging file under StorageRoot with the existing local MEK. It preserves users, roles, settings, clinical and system histories, IDs, parent relationships, and treatment states. Neither heracles.db.key nor the locally displayed recovery key changes. Installation accepts only a validated staging path produced by this SqlCipherDatabase instance. Discard deletes only an owned staging file, never a selected source.

For both transfer directions, reject selected paths resolving to the live database, development source, key blob, any of their sidecars, or the instance's staging files. Normalize Windows paths case-insensitively and reject reparse-point aliases into protected locations. A selected import source is never deleted.

Exclusive replacement and shutdown

The host's internal DatabaseMaintenanceGate and gRPC interceptor track whole admitted unary RPCs, including error paths, under a lock; ordinary calls remain concurrent. Starting maintenance atomically rejects new unary calls with Unavailable and waits asynchronously for admitted calls to finish, including multi-connection operations. Newly starting streams are rejected during maintenance; existing streams are not part of the unary drain and are cancelled or disconnected during host shutdown. No unauthenticated database-management RPC is exposed.

After preparing staging, import obtains a unary pause/drain lease with a linked 30-second timeout. Timeout or cancellation restores admission, discards staging, and refuses import without stopping the host. After a successful drain, authorization is checked again and the host queries its existing Plan repository directly, not through the paused channel. The exact active-treatment predicate is Pendingload, Partialpendingload, or Loaded. Any such plan, or lost authorization, refuses import, removes owned staging, and reopens admission. A racing external LoadForTreatment either finishes within an admitted RPC and is visible to this check or is rejected before mutation.

Once permitted, a modal busy surface prevents further user interaction. Import stops LoadForTreatmentEventSource and PlanEventSource, stops and disposes the host with a 30-second shutdown bound, shuts down the local gRPC channel, and releases SQLite pools. Once stopping starts, the host never resumes admission. Stop and Dispose are idempotent for subsequent application exit cleanup. Failure to finish shutdown or release handles gives no permission to replace the database.

Immediately before replacement, one final non-pooled keyed live connection checks journal mode. For WAL mode it performs wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE) and requires a non-busy result, then switches to journal_mode=DELETE and requires success. It closes before replacement. Checkpoint or journal-mode failure preserves the original and leads to application closure, without deleting an uncheckpointed WAL. Only obsolete live sidecars whose handles are released may be removed after successful finalization; old WAL or journal content must not accompany the replacement.

Installation uses same-volume File.Replace of the validated staging over the live database after all handles and pools are released. It never deletes the live database first, falls back to plaintext, or implements a merge. Replacement failure retains the original database. This limited production-import protection is distinct from the intentionally simple development migration.

On success, show ``Database imported. The application will close. Start it again to use the imported data.`` and invoke the existing IExitingModel.ExitApplication on the dispatcher. Do not refresh or resume old in-memory models against imported data, rotate the MEK, or automatically restart the application. An error after host shutdown begins is reported and also closes the application, even if the original remains intact; next startup opens whichever complete database was retained. App.OnExit uses references to initialized objects and does not restart stopped consumers.

Operational messages distinguish cancellation, an unavailable file or location, an invalid password or unreadable/damaged database, unsafe treatment state, and replacement failure, without including secret-bearing SQLCipher diagnostics, SQL, connection strings, recovery keys, or passwords. No unintended plaintext database copy or secret-bearing audit entry is produced. Unencrypted export files, their sibling temporary files, and selected plaintext import sources contain plaintext data and require appropriate protection. Import creates no additional plaintext copy: staging and the restored operational database remain encrypted.
